  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry and mild conditions continue through the remainder of today\nand the majority of the day tomorrow. At the surface, a ridge\nwill continue to exit the region as low pressure tracking north\nof the Great Lakes glides eastward on Sunday. The low will\neventually move a cold front east across the local forecast area\nSunday night into Monday. Rain showers associated with the cold\nfront will exit by Monday morning. Northwest flow behind the \nfront will allow for lake effect rain/snow showers to linger\nacross the Snowbelt through Monday night. Low temperatures \ntonight will settle in the upper 30s to lower 40s. Ahead of the \nfront, highs on Sunday will warm into the 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nCooler airmass moves overhead on Monday as Canadian high \npressure moves into the region. Well below normal temperatures \nare anticipated through next week with highs in the low to mid \n40s and overnight lows in the 20s early next week warming into \nthe low 30s by late next week. \n\nMedium range guidance still shows a disturbance pushing north\ninto the Ohio Valley and Great Lakes region from the Southeast\nmid to late week. There remains a fair amount of uncertainty\ngiven the spread between members regrading the timing and\nintensity of this system as it moves into the local area. For\nnow, anticipate dry conditions Tuesday and Wednesday under high\npressure with precipitation chances increasing near the\nThanksgiving holiday.",
